The given differential equation is
$\begin{array}{l}
x d y-y d x-\sqrt{x^{2}+y^{2}} d x=0 \\
\Rightarrow \quad x d y=\left(y+\sqrt{x^{2}+y^{2}}\right) d x \\
\Rightarrow \frac{d y}{d x}=\frac{\left(y+\sqrt{x^{2}+y^{2}}\right.}{x}
\end{array}
This is the linear differential equation.
Put $y=v x$, so that $\frac{d y}{d x}=v+x \frac{d v}{d x}$. Then
$\begin{array}{c}
v+x \frac{d v}{d x}=\frac{v x+\sqrt{x^{2}+v^{2} x^{2}}}{x} \\
\Rightarrow \quad v+x \frac{d v}{d x}=v+\sqrt{1+v^{2}} \\
\frac{d v}{\sqrt{1+v^{2}}}=\frac{d x}{x}
\end{array}
Integrating both sides, we get
$\int \frac{\mathrm{dv}}{\sqrt{1+\mathrm{v}^{2}}}=\int \frac{\mathrm{dx}}{\mathrm{x}}\Rightarrow  \log \left(\mathrm{v}+\sqrt{1+\mathrm{v}^{2}}\right)=\log \mathrm{x}+\log \mathrm{C}\Rightarrow\mathrm{v}+\sqrt{1+\mathrm{v}^{2}}=\mathrm{Cx}\Rightarrow \frac{\mathrm{y}}{\mathrm{x}}+\sqrt{1+\frac{\mathrm{y}^{2}}{\mathrm{x}^{2}}}=\mathrm{Cx} \quad[\because \mathrm{y}=\mathrm{vx}]\mathrm{y}+\sqrt{\mathrm{x}^{2}+\mathrm{y}^{2}}=\mathrm{Cx}^{2}$

